meta {
  name: 42 - Labels Sort Rejected
  type: graphql
  seq: 42
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  query LabelsSortRejected {
    issueLabels(first: 10, sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]) {
      nodes { id updatedAt }
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/pagination

  Pins down the one real asymmetry in this cluster. `02 - Introspect Pagination Args`
  showed `issues`, `projects` and `initiatives` each carry a `sort` argument while
  `issueLabels` does not; this request confirms that against the live API rather than
  trusting the introspection read.

  **Limitation / observed finding** (confirmed 2026-07-30): rejected.

      HTTP 400
      { "errors": [{
          "message": "Unknown argument \"sort\" on field \"Query.issueLabels\".",
          "extensions": { "code": "GRAPHQL_VALIDATION_FAILED",
                          "type": "graphql error", "userError": true,
                          "http": { "status": 400 } } }] }

  So Labels genuinely cannot sort ascending, and is locked to descending `orderBy`. That
  is the sole reason it takes a different checkpoint policy from its three siblings — see
  `40 - Labels Bare`.

  **Secondary finding, and an important one for the retry design:** this is a
  QUERY-VALIDATION error and it comes back as a real **HTTP 400**. Compare
  `06 - Page Size Over Limit`, where an argument-VALUE violation returns HTTP 200 with
  `data: null`. Linear therefore uses 400 for both permanent schema errors (this request)
  and for RATE LIMITING (`extensions.code == "RATELIMITED"`). A retry policy keyed on the
  status code alone would either retry this unfixable query forever or treat a rate limit
  as fatal. **`extensions.code`, not the HTTP status, is the only reliable discriminator.**

  **Third finding — this response carries NO rate-limit headers at all.** Note the saved
  example has no `x-complexity` / `x-ratelimit-*` block, unlike every other example in the
  collection. A GraphQL validation failure is rejected before execution, so Linear reports
  neither a complexity charge nor the remaining budget. The connector must therefore treat
  its cached rate-limit state as STALE-BUT-VALID across such an error rather than assuming
  every response refreshes the budget — reading a missing header as zero remaining would
  trigger a spurious hour-long sleep on what is really a permanent, fail-fast bug in our
  own query.
}

example {
  name: 400 — GRAPHQL_VALIDATION_FAILED: unknown argument "sort"

  request: {
    url: https://api.linear.app/graphql
    method: POST
    mode: graphql

    body:graphql: {
      query LabelsSortRejected {
        issueLabels(first: 10, sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]) {
          nodes { id updatedAt }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  response: {
    status: {
      code: 400
      text: Bad Request
    }

    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "errors": [
            {
              "message": "Unknown argument \"sort\" on field \"Query.issueLabels\".",
              "locations": [
                {
                  "line": 2,
                  "column": 26
                }
              ],
              "extensions": {
                "http": {
                  "status": 400,
                  "headers": {}
                },
                "code": "GRAPHQL_VALIDATION_FAILED",
                "type": "graphql error",
                "userError": true
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}
